Project Playwright conventions for everything under `tests/`. The constitution (`constitution.mdc`) states non-negotiables; this file shows how to apply them day to day.

**At a glance**

1. **Locators** — Prefer roles and accessible names; reserve test ids and flag CSS/XPath for review.
2. **Waiting** — Let `expect()` auto-wait; never sleep or assert on awaited booleans.
3. **Page Object Model** — Encapsulate locators and actions; keep assertions in specs.
4. **Auth** — One login per family in setup; reuse `storageState`; env-backed credentials.
5. **Test data** — Unique, self-cleaning data; no shared-environment collisions or order dependence.
6. **Tags** — One tag per `test()`; `@destructive` only for shared global state with serial workers.
7. **Test data sources** — Factories, static invalid sets, env secrets, enums for repeated strings.
8. **API** — `request` for setup/teardown and contracts; not slow UI prerequisites.
9. **Network mocking** — Simulate failure and edge responses; never mock the SUT endpoint.
10. **Accessibility** — axe on key pages plus keyboard paths; roles with names match real UI.
11. **Assertions** — Web-first `expect`; soft checks when needed; masked screenshots only.
12. **Anti-flake** — Isolation and clocks over retries/worker hacks; trace on first retry in CI.

---

## 1. Locators

Prefer locators that mirror what users see and assistive tech exposes: `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` / `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId`. Raw CSS and XPath are a smell—flag them for human review.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Save changes" }).click();
await page.getByLabel("Work email").fill(email);
await page.getByPlaceholder("Search courses").fill(query);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator("#save-btn").click();
await page.locator('//button[contains(@class,"primary")]').click();
```

**Why:** Role- and name-based locators survive restyling and match accessibility contracts; CSS/XPath tie tests to implementation detail.

---

## 2. Waiting

Playwright’s web-first assertions retry until timeout. Never guess time with `waitForTimeout`, and never await a boolean then assert it.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole("heading", { name: "Overview" })).toBeVisible();
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/dashboard/);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
await expect(await row.isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Fixed delays race the app; snapshot booleans skip Playwright’s built-in waiting and flake under load.

---

## 3. Page Object Model

One POM per page or meaningful component. Define locators in the constructor; methods express user actions only. Specs own assertions.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
export class SettingsPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
    this.displayName = page.getByLabel("Display name");
  }
  private readonly displayName: Locator;

  /** @param name New display name to submit */
  async updateDisplayName(name: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.displayName.fill(name);
    await this.page.getByRole("button", { name: "Save" }).click();
  }
}

// spec
await settings.updateDisplayName(uniqueName);
await expect(page.getByText(uniqueName)).toBeVisible();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
export class SettingsPage extends BasePage {
  async assertSaved(): Promise<void> {
    await expect(this.page.getByText("Saved")).toBeVisible();
  }
}
```

**Why:** POMs stay reusable action layers; assertions in specs keep failure messages close to the scenario under test.

