## 5. Test data

Every test creates data it can identify uniquely (factory or `Date.now()` suffix) and tears it down via API or UI. Tests must not depend on run order or on another student’s rows in the shared environment.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
const title = `Assignment ${Date.now()}`;
await api.post("/assignments", { data: { title } });
test.info().attach("assignmentId", { body: id });
// afterEach: DELETE via request fixture
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const title = "My assignment"; // same every run
test.describe.configure({ mode: "serial" });
test("B updates shared row", async () => { /* assumes test A ran */ });
```

**Why:** Parallel CI and shared tenants need isolation; unique data plus cleanup prevents cross-talk and order coupling.

---

## 6. Tags

Exactly one tag per `test()`: `@smoke` (every PR), `@sanity` (every push), `@regression` (full suite on manual dispatch), `@api`, `@e2e`, `@destructive` (only when mutating **shared/global** state both families rely on—run with `--workers=1`). Never tag `describe()`. Tests that only touch their own data are not `@destructive`. Any state-mutating test reverts in `afterEach` / `afterAll`.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test("lists courses after filter @sanity", async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });

test("disables org-wide feature flag @destructive", async ({ request }) => {
  // mutates settings everyone shares; restore in afterAll
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe("Checkout", { tag: "@smoke" }, () => {
  test("pays @smoke @regression", async () => { /* two tags */ });
});
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI selection; describe-level or duplicate tags skew reporting, and `@destructive` must mean truly shared blast radius.

