## 8. API

Use the `request` fixture for setup, teardown, and contract checks. When a backend endpoint exists, do not create prerequisite data by clicking through the UI.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test("shows imported row @api", async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { id } = await createRowViaApi(request);
  await page.goto(`/rows/${id}`);
  await expect(page.getByRole("cell", { name: id })).toBeVisible();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test("shows imported row @e2e", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/import");
  // 12 UI steps to seed one row before the assertion under test
});
```

**Why:** API setup is faster, clearer, and failures point at the layer you intend to test.

---

## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` / `route.fulfill` to simulate 500, 503, timeout, empty lists, or malformed payloads. Never mock the endpoint whose behavior the test is meant to validate.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.route("**/notifications", (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: "Service unavailable" }),
);
await page.goto("/inbox");
await expect(page.getByRole("alert")).toContainText("try again");
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
// Test title: "POST /enroll persists seat"
await page.route("**/enroll", (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ ok: true }) }),
);
```

**Why:** Mock dependencies and failure modes, not the contract you are trying to prove.

